# Swift / SwiftUI Rules (Fit33 iOS)

These rules only load when editing Swift files under `Fit33/`. Cross-cutting rules (Agent Team Protocol, general principles, deploy shortcuts) live in `codingrules.mdc`.

## Unit Preferences (km / mi)
- **Strava and any cardio distance UI MUST honor `UnitSettingsManager.shared.distanceUnit`.** The Strava API speaks meters and seconds-per-km; never render those raw or hardcode `"km"` / `"/km"` / `"%.1f m"` in any user-facing label. Use the canonical helpers on `UnitSettingsManager`: `formatStravaDistance(meters:)`, `formatStravaPace(secondsPerKm:)`, `formatStravaElevation(meters:)`, `stravaDistanceValue(meters:)` for charts, `stravaDistanceShortLabel` ("mi"/"km"), `stravaPaceUnitLabel` ("/mi"/"/km").
- **Reactive updates**: every Strava view that displays distance/pace/elevation MUST observe the manager via `@ObservedObject private var unitSettings = UnitSettingsManager.shared` (the singleton is `ObservableObject`). For charts, recompute data points inside `.onChange(of: unitSettings.distanceUnit)` so bars/axes flip immediately when the user toggles units in Settings. Pattern: `DashboardStravaWidget`, `StravaSettingsView`, `StravaStatsCharts`, `CardioStravaSection`, `StravaActivityRecapSheet`.
- The cloud-synced source of truth is `user_profiles.distance_unit` (`'miles'` / `'km'`); locale default is `imperial` for `US/LR/MM`, otherwise metric. Do NOT add a Strava-specific unit toggle — there is one global preference.

## Code Quality
- **Logging**: ALWAYS use `AppLogger` (from `Logger.swift`) — NEVER `print()`. Categories: `.network`, `.data`, `.workout`, `.social`, `.nutrition`, `.health`, `.ui`, `.performance`, `.auth`, `.general`. **Phase 12 (2026-04-25)**: every `AppLogger` convenience method (`verbose`/`debug`/`info`/`warning`/`error`/`critical`) takes default `file: String = #file, function: String = #function, line: Int = #line` and forwards them through to `log(...)`, which writes them into `AdvancedSessionLogger`'s `extra:` dict as `x_file`/`x_line`/`x_function`. The hourly Supabase cron `bug_intel_backfill_callsites` reads those keys and writes them onto `bug_intelligence_fingerprints.last_seen_file/function/line` — Claude's `triage-bugs` Edge Function then locks `file_path` to that authoritative call-site. Do NOT add a new Swift logging entry point that bypasses `AppLogger.log` — losing call-site context drops triage accuracy from "exact file:line" back to Claude's heuristic guess.
- **Network/Supabase catch blocks MUST use `NetworkErrorClassifier`**: In every `catch { … }` that wraps a Supabase / URLSession / Combine-network call, call `NetworkErrorClassifier.log(error, context: "…", category: .x)` (from `NetworkErrorClassifier.swift`) instead of `AppLogger.error(...)`. `AppLogger.error` auto-routes to `CrashReportingService.reportError` AND writes a `dev_session_logs.entries[type=error]` row — that pair creates a `bug_intelligence_fingerprint` per occurrence. The classifier keeps transient (NSURLError timeout/cancelled/network-lost), expected HealthKit (protected/no-data/auth-not-determined), and auth-expired/RLS-violation failures at `.warning`/`.debug` while reserving `.error` for real malfunctions. Optional `transientLevel: .debug` for retry-covered paths (dashboard fetches, offline queue flush). **Automated lint**: `scripts/classifier_lint.py` scans every Supabase-touching catch block and warns via `.github/workflows/classifier-lint.yml` + `.githooks/pre-commit`. Suppress a legit exception (e.g. bootstrap before auth) with `// classifier_lint:allow` inside the catch block. The lint starts in warn-only mode and flips to `--strict` once the existing backlog is cleared.
- **`op:` arguments must match `PerformanceSignposts.Op.rawValue`**: Every `op:` parameter to `NetworkErrorClassifier.log`, `DiagnosticContext(op:)`, or `PerformanceSignposts.measure` must reference a canonical op — either `Op.xxx.rawValue` or a string literal that exists in the enum. `Fit33Tests/PerformanceSignpostsCoverageTests` enforces this. Typoed ops splinter `bug_intelligence_fingerprints.structural_fingerprint` (added by migration 20260516) and break the "same root cause, different call sites" collapse.
- **No force unwraps**: Never use `!` on optionals in production code. Use `guard let`, `if let`, or `??`.
- **Design tokens**: Use `.ds_*` font tokens from `DesignSystem.swift` and `Color.cardBackground` from `AdaptiveColors.swift`. NEVER hardcode `.font(.system(size:))` or define local `cardBackground` properties.
- **Structured concurrency**: Use `Task { }` with `Task.sleep(for:)` — NEVER `DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter`.
- **Accessibility**: All new interactive elements must have `.accessibilityLabel()` and `.accessibilityHint()`. Decorative elements get `.accessibilityHidden(true)`. Interactive elements need a ≥44×44pt hit area — pad small visuals (page dots, icons) with `.padding` + `.contentShape(Rectangle())` rather than growing the visual.
- **No `UIScreen.main.bounds` for layout** (2026-07-31): screen bounds ≠ window size in iPad split view / Stage Manager. Use `GeometryReader` (and cap widths where a phone-designed panel would balloon).
- **Thread safety**: `@Published` properties must only be mutated on the main thread. Use `@MainActor`, `NSLock`, or actor isolation for shared mutable state.
- **`@Published` + non-`@MainActor` ObservableObjects (Q2-82)**: Classes that do heavy network / Core Data work (`SupabaseManager`, `ExerciseLibraryService`, `UserManager`) are intentionally NOT marked `@MainActor` because it would serialize background work onto main. Every `@Published` assignment in them MUST be wrapped in `await MainActor.run { … }`. When adding a new `@Published` var, audit every write site before merging. Opposite end of the spectrum — small UI-only `ObservableObject`s (`RestTimer`) SHOULD be `@MainActor` to codify the contract.

## Stability & Performance
- **No sync Core Data in `init()` or `.task`**: Never call `context.fetch()` or `context.execute()` synchronously on the main thread during app startup. Use `context.perform { }` or a background context (`container.newBackgroundContext()`).
- **Auth guard before RPCs**: Every Supabase RPC call (especially from background sync paths like `syncHealthKitDataToChallenges`) MUST check `SupabaseManager.shared.isAuthenticated` first. Server-side `auth.uid()` returns NULL on expired sessions, causing "Not authenticated" errors that multiply through retry logic.
- **JSONSerialization safety**: ALWAYS call `JSONSerialization.isValidJSONObject(obj)` before `JSONSerialization.data(withJSONObject:)`. `try?`/`try` does NOT catch Objective-C `NSInvalidArgumentException` — it bypasses Swift error handling entirely and causes SIGABRT. Critical for MetricKit payloads (`MXMetricPayload.jsonRepresentation()`).
- **HealthKit error classification**: Errors containing "Protected health data", "No data available", or "authorization not determined" are EXPECTED (device locked, no samples yet). Log at `.debug`, never `.error`. Use case-insensitive matching (`lowercased().contains(...)`) — wording varies across iOS versions. Prefer `NetworkErrorClassifier.log(...)` which already encodes these patterns.
- **`MainThreadWatchdog` + `ProductionFPSMonitor` are DEBUG-only**: Both are gated by `#if DEBUG` internally (inside `start()`), AND by the caller (`Fit33App.swift`). Release builds rely on `MetricKitSubscriber` for hang/crash diagnostics. Adding a new call site without a `#if DEBUG` gate will regress by producing 20+ false-positive `bug_intelligence_fingerprints` per cold-start (initial 2-3s Dashboard load = "freeze"). (2026-05-03: the `PerformanceOptimizationsInitializer` wrapper that previously bundled this start was removed — Fit33App is now the only canonical caller.)
- **Heavy sync work off main actor**: Functions like `generateMotivationalMessage()` (~300 lines) must run via `Task.detached` when called from `.task` (which runs on main actor). `WorkoutSuggestionEngine` is intentionally NOT `@MainActor` — it uses a private `bgContext` with `performAndWait` so it can run on any thread.
- **Dashboard `.task` parallelism**: Dashboard initial load fires ALL independent work as separate `Task {}` blocks — recommendations, motivational message, cardio, health sync, and social fetches run in parallel. Auth wait uses a Combine publisher on `$isAuthenticated` (no polling). All 14 social/challenge/quest calls run in a single `async let` group.
- **Startup timing**: Critical startup paths (auth check, limitations fetch, cloud sync) should log elapsed milliseconds via `CFAbsoluteTimeGetCurrent()` so regressions are visible in crash reports.
- **Long-lived observers MUST be retained and torn down on sign-out** (2026-07-30): any `HKObserverQuery` (or similar registration) must be stored so it can be `healthStore.stop(...)`ped later. Pattern: `BackgroundChallengeSyncService.activeObserverQueries` + `teardownForSignOut()`, called from `SupabaseManager.signOut()`/`deleteAccount()`. Fire-and-forget observer registration leaks queries across accounts on the same device.
- **Failed cloud saves of workout data go through `CloudSyncRetryQueue`** — strength uses `enqueueWorkoutCloudSync`, cardio uses `enqueueCardioCloudSync(payload)` (added 2026-07-30). Never leave a `catch` that only logs on a durable-data save path; enqueue for retry instead.

## Workout Engine Invariants (2026-07-31 polish sprint)
- **Completion fanout is once-only and gated on a durable save.** Strength: `WorkoutManager.didRunCompletionFanout` short-circuits repeat `finishWorkout()` calls. Cardio: XP/quest/league/feed fanout runs ONLY when the save returned a real workout ID; otherwise enqueue `CloudSyncRetryQueue` (entries carry `fanoutCompleted` so the retry path fans out exactly once). Never add a second call site that awards XP/points for the same workout.
- **Set weights are stored as TOTAL POUNDS.** All display↔storage conversion (kg toggle, per-side "each side" exercises) goes through the `totalLbs(fromDisplayWeight:)` / `displayWeight(fromTotalLbs:)` pair in `WorkoutSetViews.swift`. Never inline `* 2.20462` / per-side math at a call site — that's how the double-conversion corruption (finding A) shipped.
- **Active-workout state persistence goes through `WorkoutManager`'s serial `statePersistenceQueue`.** Both saves AND `clearActiveWorkoutStorage()` — a direct UserDefaults write can be reordered against an in-flight save and resurrect a cleared workout.
- **Never `cancelWorkout()` on timeout/recovery paths** — it deletes completed sets. Salvage instead: persist completed sets, enqueue cloud sync, then clear state (`salvageTimedOutWorkout()` pattern) and tell the user via the `WorkoutAutoEnded` notification.
- **Per-slot active-workout UI state (swap counts, shuffled-exercise IDs) lives on `WorkoutManager`** (`slotSwapCounts` / `slotShuffledExerciseIds`), not view `@State` — cards are recreated on scroll and `@State` resets silently.
- **PR and progression queries MUST exclude warmup sets** (`setType != .warmup` / `"Warmup"`), matching how they're excluded from volume.

## Singleton `init()` & UserDefaults
- **No `UserDefaults.synchronize()`**: iOS auto-batches writes. `.synchronize()` forces synchronous disk I/O — never call it.
- **Singleton `init()` must return instantly**: Core Data fetches and heavy work in service `init()` MUST be wrapped in `Task {}` or `Task { @MainActor in }`. Pattern: `MealService`, `CloudProgramService`, `SmartProgramEngine`.
- **Exception**: UserDefaults reads + small JSON decodes (<50KB) ARE safe and encouraged synchronously in `init()` to populate `@Published` properties before the first SwiftUI frame (instant UI). Pattern: `DailyQuestService.loadCachedQuests()`, `ChallengeService.loadCachedChallengesSync()`.
- **Computed properties in SwiftUI body MUST NOT fetch Core Data**: Cache results from async background fetches. `getUserStoredWeight()` pattern: return cached value instantly, kick off async fetch to populate cache.

## View Composition / Widget Isolation
- **No duplicate fetches in `.onAppear`**: If data is already loaded in `.task(id:)` on the same view, do NOT duplicate the fetch in `.onAppear` — `.onAppear` fires on every tab switch back. Use `.onChange(scenePhase)` for foreground resume instead.
- **Isolate `ObservableObject` subscriptions in wrapper views**: When a parent view subscribes to many services, a change in ANY service recomputes the entire body. Fix: convert parent subscriptions to plain `let` references and wrap sections that need live updates in small independent `View` structs owning their own `@StateObject`. Example: `DashboardQuestsWrapper`.
- **Widget isolation rule (mandatory for new features)**: Any widget in a `ScrollView` with 5+ siblings MUST be its own `View` struct owning its service subscriptions (`@StateObject`/`@ObservedObject`). Parent views must NEVER read `@EnvironmentObject`/`@ObservedObject` properties inline for widget rendering — pass only stable values (bindings, constants, cached `@State`) to isolated wrappers. Pattern: `DashboardQuestsWrapper`, `DashboardHeaderWrapper`, `DashboardChallengesWrapper` in `DashboardView+Helpers.swift`.
- **`@FetchRequest` must have `fetchLimit`**: When a view only displays N items (e.g. `prefix(10)`), set `fetchLimit: N`. Without it Core Data fetches ALL matching rows and every row change triggers view invalidation. Dashboard uses `fetchLimit: 10`.
- **Horizontal `DragGesture` in vertical `ScrollView`**: Use `.simultaneousGesture(DragGesture(minimumDistance: 25))` — NOT `.highPriorityGesture` with minimumDistance 8. High priority with low distance steals touches from vertical scroll.

## Widget / Watch Extension Auth (App Group session blob)
- **The Supabase session blob in App Group `UserDefaults` is camelCase, NOT snake_case.** `supabase-swift` 2.40's internal `Sources/Auth/Internal/SessionStorage.swift` `store(_:)` uses a plain `JSONEncoder()` with **no** `keyEncodingStrategy` — even though `AuthClient.Configuration.jsonEncoder` (in `Defaults.swift`) is configured with `.convertToSnakeCase`, that configured encoder is NOT used by `SessionStorage.live`. Net result: the on-disk blob has top-level keys `accessToken`, `refreshToken`, `expiresAt`, `expiresIn`, `tokenType`, `user` — never `access_token`. Any extension-side decoder that defines a `MinimalSession` looking for `access_token` will throw `DecodingError.keyNotFound` and surface as `malformedSession` / `notAuthenticated`, leaving the widget stuck on stale bytes (we shipped this bug 2026-04-27 — silent until users tapped the refresh affordance).
- **Canonical key**: `supabase.session.fit33.supabase.session.v1` in `UserDefaults(suiteName: "group.com.fit33.app")`. NEVER hardcode `fit33.widget.supabase.session.v1` — that's a stale older key. Single source of truth lives in `Fit33/SupabaseAppGroupStorage.swift` (`SupabaseAppGroupStorage.userDefaultsKey`).
- **Required decoder shape**: every extension-side `MinimalSession` MUST tolerate both `accessToken` and `access_token` to stay resilient to future supabase-swift SDK bumps (and to past versions if a user's blob predates the fix). Pattern in `RunningActivityWidget/WidgetSupabaseFetcher.swift` and `Fit33Watch Watch App/WatchAppGroupSession.swift`:
  ```swift
  struct MinimalSession: Decodable {
      let accessToken: String
      private enum CodingKeys: String, CodingKey {
          case accessToken
          case access_token  // swiftlint:disable:this identifier_name
      }
      init(from decoder: Decoder) throws {
          let c = try decoder.container(keyedBy: CodingKeys.self)
          if let t = try c.decodeIfPresent(String.self, forKey: .accessToken) { self.accessToken = t }
          else if let t = try c.decodeIfPresent(String.self, forKey: .access_token) { self.accessToken = t }
          else { throw DecodingError.keyNotFound(CodingKeys.accessToken, .init(codingPath: c.codingPath, debugDescription: "neither accessToken nor access_token present")) }
      }
  }
  ```
- **Do NOT** call `JSONDecoder().keyDecodingStrategy = .convertFromSnakeCase` on the App-Group blob — the keys are already camelCase, so the strategy becomes a no-op AND silently breaks if the SDK ever flips back to snake_case. The dual-key custom `init(from:)` above is the only forward-compatible shape.
- **App-side storage rule**: `Fit33/SupabaseAppGroupStorage.swift` is a pass-through `AuthLocalStorage` — it stores/retrieves the raw `Data` blob from `supabase-swift` without re-encoding. Do not add a re-encode step "to fix the casing" — that would mutate the blob the SDK expects to read back unchanged on the main app side and break session restore on app launch.
- **Diagnostic**: `WidgetTapLog.captureEnvironment()` in `RunningActivityWidget/RefreshChallengeIntent.swift` reads the same `UserDefaults` key as `WidgetSessionStorage` — keep them in sync. A divergence here was the 2026-04-27 root cause: tap log reported `hasJWT=true` while the fetcher couldn't decode the same blob.

## OAuth Token Persistence (Keychain write verification)
- **`KeychainHelper.save` is `SecItemUpdate`-first, `SecItemAdd` on `errSecItemNotFound` (atomic).** Rewritten 2026-04-29 — the prior `SecItemDelete` → `SecItemAdd` sequence had a non-atomic "delete window" where a transient `errSecInteractionNotAllowed` between the two calls left the keychain entry permanently empty. Atomic `SecItemUpdate` leaves the prior value intact on failure; the add path now only runs for brand-new entries. Do NOT replace this pattern with `delete + add` again — it regresses the silent-token-loss bug that produced "WHOOP card disappeared on cold launch with no `disconnect()` log line" (Joe Reed incident 2026-04-29).
- **Rotated OAuth access AND refresh tokens MUST be persisted via `KeychainHelper.saveAndVerify`, not the property setter.** Both directions are vulnerable. `KeychainHelper.save(key:value:)` returns `OSStatus`; the property setters in `WhoopService` / `OuraService` / `StravaService` / `FitbitService` discard the status because Swift property setters can't propagate failure. After every successful OAuth `/oauth/token` response (both initial connect via `exchangeCodeForTokens` AND every rotation in `_performTokenRefresh`), call `writeAccessTokenVerified(tokenResponse.accessToken)` and (when present) `writeRefreshTokenVerified(tokenResponse.refreshToken)`. On failure: log warning, retry once; if the retry also fails, on the connect path THROW `tokenExchangeFailed` (don't declare a phantom-connected state); on the refresh path THROW `tokenRefreshFailed` (the prior AT remains atomic-intact thanks to `SecItemUpdate`-first). Canonical incidents: bug-intel `af583196` (RT wiped, 13 occ — fixed 24e5efd) and Joe Reed 2026-04-29 (AT wiped silently, no log line — fixed by this rule).auth/token` response that includes a rotated `refresh_token`, call `KeychainHelper.saveAndVerify(key:, value:)` (returns `Bool`); if the round-trip fails, retry once, then accept that the previous (still-valid) RT remains in the keychain — the next refresh attempt will surface the canonical disconnect path with a clear cause instead of silently signing the user out via "rt missing". `WhoopService._performTokenRefresh` is the canonical reference implementation; mirror it in any new wearable OAuth integration. NEVER add a new keychain-backed OAuth integration that uses the silent property-setter shape for the rotated RT — it WILL regress the same bug the day a user wakes the app on a locked device.
- **`KeychainHelper.save` and `KeychainHelper.delete` now return `OSStatus`** (with `@discardableResult` so existing call sites stay binary-compatible). Failures log at `.warning` so the breadcrumb survives even when callers `_ = save(...)`. Bookkeeping / migration call sites can keep ignoring the return; OAuth refresh paths MUST check it (or use `saveAndVerify`).
- **OAuth disconnect log lines must be `.warning`, not `.error`.** Every `disconnect()` lands here including intentional user "Disconnect" taps and the silent recovery path triggered by `rt_wiped_keychain_readable`. None of those are bug-class events — logging them at `.error` inflates bug-intel severity scores and forces the fingerprint into the high-severity export queue every time it fires. Real OAuth failures (HTTP 4xx token reject, network timeout) are still logged at `.error` in the calling path BEFORE they reach `disconnect()`.

## Core Data Context Safety
- **NEVER use service singleton caches inside `bgContext.perform`**: `ExerciseLibraryService.shared.getExercise(byName:)` and `.getAllExercises()` return viewContext objects. Inside `bgContext.perform`, always use `bgContext.fetch()` to get objects.
- **NEVER use `getAllExercises()` on a non-`@MainActor` class**: The returned viewContext objects are unsafe to access from background threads. Use pre-extracted cache data (e.g., `exerciseAnalysisCache` in `SmartExercisePairingEngine`) for background work; resolve names to `Exercise` objects only for final results.
- **`bgContext.mergePolicy` is MANDATORY**: Every `newBackgroundContext()` that sets `automaticallyMergesChangesFromParent = true` MUST also set `bgContext.mergePolicy = NSMergeByPropertyObjectTrumpMergePolicy`. Without it, concurrent parent context saves can produce cross-context object references that crash on relationship assignment.
- **`Task { }` on `@MainActor` types inherits main actor**: Use `Task.detached` for work that must run off main thread. `Task { [self] in }` on an `@MainActor` class runs ON main.
- **DEBUG context guard**: Use `#if DEBUG exercise?.assertContext(bgContext) #endif` before setting relationships in background context blocks (helper defined in `PersistenceController.swift`).
- **Every `Transformable` Core Data attribute MUST declare `customClassName="<Class>"` AND every assignment site MUST cast to that exact class.** Without `customClassName`, `NSSecureUnarchiveFromDataTransformer`-backed attributes log `Transformable attribute missing customClassName` warnings on every store load (see `PersistenceController.swift:326` `coredata.transformable_scan`) and modern iOS may refuse to deserialize them. The class name MUST match the transformer's `transformedValueClass()` — `StringArrayValueTransformer.transformedValueClass()` returns `NSArray.self`, so its three attributes (`Exercise.muscleGroups`, `Exercise.secondaryMuscles`, `User.equipment` in `Fit33/DataModel.xcdatamodeld/.../contents`) declare `customClassName="NSArray"`. **Cascade hazard**: declaring `customClassName="NSArray"` changes the generated property type from `Any?` to `NSArray?`, so every `… as NSObject` assignment site stops compiling (`Cannot assign value of type 'NSObject' to type 'NSArray?'`). When tightening the model, sweep the codebase for `as NSObject` and rewrite to `as NSArray` (or drop the cast — `[String]` bridges automatically). Canonical fix touched 19 sites across `SupabaseManager`, `ExerciseLibraryService`, `PersistenceController`, `CoreDataExtensions`, `UserManager`, `CustomWorkoutBuilderView`, `ProfileView`, `Fit33App`, and `Fit33App` (2026-04-25). When adding a new `Transformable` attribute: (1) write or reuse a `NSSecureUnarchiveFromDataTransformer` subclass; (2) set its `transformedValueClass()`; (3) declare the SAME class via `customClassName=` on the XML attribute; (4) cast every assignment as that class — never `as NSObject`.
- **`SmartExercisePairingEngine` is NOT `@MainActor`**: Uses a background Core Data context for exercise fetches in `buildPairingDatabase()`. The old pattern of `MainActor.run { getAllExercises() }` caused 6fps drops by fetching 5500+ exercises on the main thread.

## Video / AVFoundation
- **AVFoundation object creation MUST happen off main thread**: `AVURLAsset`, `AVPlayerItem`, `AVQueuePlayer`, `AVPlayerLooper` construction must use `Task.detached`. Only final `@Published` assignment uses `MainActor.run`.
- **`GenderFilterService.getVideoFilename()` has O(n) fuzzy fallback**: Never call from the main thread in a hot path. Use `Task.detached` when calling from video load methods.

## Social / Profile Architecture
- **`ProfileUser`** is the universal model for displaying any user profile (friend or non-friend). Defined in `FriendProfileView.swift`. Has convenience inits from `Friend`, `UserSearchResult`, `SuggestedFriend`, `LeagueEntry`, `CommunityLeaderboardEntry`, `FriendActivity`.
- **`FriendProfileView`** accepts `ProfileUser` (via `init(user:)` or `init(friend:)`). Shows full profile for friends, streamlined card (photo/name/goal/add friend/block) for non-friends. Never create a separate profile view — extend this one.
- **Daily Goals** (`DailyQuestService`) must never return empty. Every code path (no auth, server error, empty response) falls back to `defaultGoals()`. The generic "Daily Quests" placeholder was removed — real goals always show.

## Dashboard Notification Cards
- **Unified carousel**: All dashboard notification types (friend requests, received workouts, 1v1/group/private challenge invites) live in a single `DashboardNotificationCarousel` in `DashboardModels.swift`. Do NOT add separate notification containers to `DashboardView` — add new notification types to the `NotificationItem` enum in the carousel.
- **Card styling**: All notification cards use `staticCardBackground(accentColor:secondaryColor:)` (defined in `ReceivedWorkoutPreviewWidget.swift`) — a static raised-card effect matching active challenge widgets. No animated `AngularGradient` glow on notification cards.
- **Sort order**: Friend requests always first (highest priority), then all others sorted oldest-first by date.

## Daily Quest / Challenge Integration (app-side)
- **Step quest challenge sync**: When a user has an active step challenge, the `get_daily_quests` RPC receives `p_active_step_challenge_target` from the app. Swift sends this from `ChallengeService.shared.activeChallenges` and `activeGroupChallenges` (challenge types: `"steps"`, `"walk"`).
- **Notification type consistency**: Push notifications from `accept_friend_request` use `friend_accepted`; in-app `app_notifications` rows use `friend_request_accepted`. `NotificationManager.handleNotificationType` must handle BOTH strings. The `app_notifications` table schema uses `reference_id` (UUID) and `from_user_id` (UUID), NOT a `data` JSONB column.

## Weekly League (app-side `not_placed` handling)
- **Placement is Monday-only** (enforced server-side). `get_or_join_weekly_league` returns `{"not_placed": true}` for unplaced users on Tuesday–Sunday.
- **Swift handling**: `WeeklyLeagueService.notPlaced` + `notPlacedTierName` + `notPlacedNextWeek`. Widget shows "League Starts Monday" state via `notPlacedContent` in `WeeklyLeagueViews.swift`.

## Main Tab Navigation
- **Current pattern**: All five main tabs use **hidden system nav bar** + **custom in-layout headers** (see `DESIGN_AGENT.md` → *iOS 26 Liquid Glass Readiness* and `LEGACY_CUSTOM_HEADERS.md`).
- **Optional**: `floatingTopBarLeading` / `floatingTopBarActiveWorkoutTimer()` in `DesignSystem.swift` support a system-toolbar + Liquid Glass style if you switch back.

## Rage-shake screen tracking (mandatory for every new View)
- **Every new top-level View struct that becomes a navigation destination MUST be tracked in the rage-shake pipeline.** When a user shakes to report a bug, `BugReportView` reads `SessionLogManager.shared.getCurrentScreenInfo()` + passes the result to `ScreenCodeMap.filesForScreen(...)`. Without tracking, Claude receives an empty screen name and falls back to the last-tracked screen — users see "Files Claude will review: Fit33/ProfileView.swift" while standing on a totally different page (the 2026-04-24 rage-shake bug). Three things must ship in the SAME PR when adding a new screen:
  1. **Add a `case` to `SessionLogManager.Screen`** with a stable `S`-prefixed id (pick the next free id in the appropriate range — e.g. social is `S95X`, workout is `S3XX`). Add the `displayName: String` switch arm at the same time.
  2. **Add `.trackScreen(.<case>, metadata: [...])` to the view's body.** Place it alongside `.navigationTitle(...)` / `.navigationBarTitleDisplayMode(...)` so the tracking fires on appear + tears down on disappear. Include stable metadata (challenge id, user id, workout id, etc.) if the view is parameterized — future triage can correlate the rage-shake to the specific entity.
  3. **Add a `ScreenCodeMap.table` entry keyed by the display name**, listing view file + nearby helper views + direct service dependencies + shared models. Aim for 5-10 files so Claude has real context. The foundational-files list (SupabaseManager / UserManager / NetworkErrorClassifier / Logger / DiagnosticContext) is always appended automatically — don't re-list those.
- **`Fit33Tests/ScreenCodeMapCoverageTests`** enforces the invariant: `testEveryScreenCaseHasAMapEntry` fails the build if a new `Screen` case has no map entry, and `testEveryMappedFileExistsOnDisk` fails if any referenced file was renamed/deleted. CI catches the regression at PR time instead of at user shake time.
- **Screen enum cases are append-only.** Never rename an existing case — downstream `SessionLogManager` persists them as raw string ids into `dev_session_logs`, and renaming breaks historical analytics joins. To deprecate, leave the case, document the replacement in the displayName comment, and route it to the new display name via a parallel map entry.

## Build Phase Lint
- **`scripts/perf_lint.sh`** runs automatically on every build (added to Xcode Build Phases). Catches `UserDefaults.synchronize()`, cross-context risks, `performAndWait`, and sync Core Data in `init()`. Errors fail the build; warnings require manual review.

## File Organization
- For NEW code: keep files under 200–300 lines when practical.
- Extract reusable components to separate files (follow `OnboardingComponents.swift`).
- For large views, use `extension ViewName` in separate files (e.g., `DashboardView+Header.swift`) — keep all stored properties in the main struct, methods in focused extension files.
- Do NOT refactor existing large files unless explicitly requested — too risky for a working app. Exception: performance-critical extraction of wrapper views from large SwiftUI views when explicitly requested.
